1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,900
Sunday night.

2
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:02,700
Bo's garage is now a headquarters.

3
00:00:03,360 --> 00:00:05,680
There's a whiteboard, a laptop, and a bag

4
00:00:05,680 --> 00:00:08,060
of crisps nobody is allowed to touch.

5
00:00:08,760 --> 00:00:10,620
Kai has been watching the rock every night.

6
00:00:11,100 --> 00:00:11,880
Now Kai has numbers.

7
00:00:12,560 --> 00:00:13,580
Kai turns the laptop around.

8
00:00:13,980 --> 00:00:16,000
On the screen there's a dot, and a

9
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:17,220
long curved line.

10
00:00:17,840 --> 00:00:19,220
The line ends at Earth.

11
00:00:19,920 --> 00:00:22,100
It's about a hundred and fifty metres wide,

12
00:00:22,380 --> 00:00:22,700
Kai says.

13
00:00:23,440 --> 00:00:25,060
And it's heading for us.

14
00:00:26,200 --> 00:00:27,020
Nobody says anything.

15
00:00:27,500 --> 00:00:28,360
Ravi takes off his headphones.

16
00:00:28,360 --> 00:00:31,520
So what if an asteroid was heading for

17
00:00:31,520 --> 00:00:31,720
Earth?

18
00:00:32,120 --> 00:00:32,580
A real one.

19
00:00:32,580 --> 00:00:34,060
With your name on it.

20
00:00:35,080 --> 00:00:35,900
First, the speed.

21
00:00:36,380 --> 00:00:38,420
Asteroids hit Earth at about twenty kilometres a

22
00:00:38,420 --> 00:00:38,720
second.

23
00:00:38,980 --> 00:00:40,720
That's from London to Paris in under twenty

24
00:00:40,720 --> 00:00:41,140
seconds.

25
00:00:41,680 --> 00:00:43,200
And speed is where the energy hides.

26
00:00:43,620 --> 00:00:45,400
A rock this size, at that speed, carries

27
00:00:45,400 --> 00:00:47,500
about a hundred and seventy megatons.

28
00:00:47,800 --> 00:00:49,560
That's more than three times the biggest bomb

29
00:00:49,560 --> 00:00:50,780
humans ever tested.

30
00:00:51,560 --> 00:00:52,320
Bo cracks her knuckles.

31
00:00:52,880 --> 00:00:53,520
Kai shakes their head.

32
00:00:54,320 --> 00:00:54,960
Not even you.

33
00:00:56,340 --> 00:00:58,100
Even small ones are scary.

34
00:00:58,760 --> 00:01:01,140
In 2013, over the city of Chelyabinsk in

35
00:01:01,140 --> 00:01:03,540
Russia, a rock about eighteen metres across hit

36
00:01:03,540 --> 00:01:03,940
the air.

37
00:01:04,280 --> 00:01:06,420
It exploded above the city with about thirty

38
00:01:06,420 --> 00:01:08,360
times the energy of the Hiroshima bomb.

39
00:01:08,860 --> 00:01:10,820
Around fifteen hundred people were hurt.

40
00:01:11,260 --> 00:01:12,920
Almost all of them by glass, when the

41
00:01:12,920 --> 00:01:14,340
shock wave blew in their windows.

42
00:01:14,720 --> 00:01:16,080
People ran to their windows to see the

43
00:01:16,080 --> 00:01:16,420
flash.

44
00:01:16,700 --> 00:01:18,100
Then the windows came to them.

45
00:01:19,320 --> 00:01:21,640
And sixty-six million years ago, a rock about

46
00:01:21,640 --> 00:01:24,140
ten kilometres wide hit what is now Mexico.

47
00:01:24,640 --> 00:01:25,960
You know how that ended for the dinosaurs.

48
00:01:26,900 --> 00:01:28,440
Ravi asks the obvious question.

49
00:01:28,980 --> 00:01:30,560
How did nobody see that one coming?

50
00:01:31,200 --> 00:01:32,020
Easy, Kai says.

51
00:01:32,420 --> 00:01:33,260
Nobody had telescopes.

52
00:01:34,540 --> 00:01:35,060
The good news?

53
00:01:35,620 --> 00:01:37,320
Astronomers watch the sky all the time.

54
00:01:37,720 --> 00:01:40,360
On the thirteenth of April, 2029, an asteroid

55
00:01:40,360 --> 00:01:43,300
called Apophis will fly past Earth closer than

56
00:01:43,300 --> 00:01:44,220
some of our satellites.

57
00:01:44,640 --> 00:01:45,120
It won't hit.

58
00:01:45,360 --> 00:01:45,680
We checked.

59
00:01:45,680 --> 00:01:46,560
Twice.

60
00:01:48,420 --> 00:01:50,460
Back in the garage, Bo wants to punch

61
00:01:50,460 --> 00:01:50,740
it.

62
00:01:51,120 --> 00:01:52,280
Nia wants to fly up and look at

63
00:01:52,280 --> 00:01:52,480
it.

64
00:01:52,880 --> 00:01:54,200
Ravi wants to try pushing it.

65
00:01:54,800 --> 00:01:56,500
Kai reminds Nia that there's no air up

66
00:01:56,500 --> 00:01:58,640
there, and it's much colder than her worst

67
00:01:58,640 --> 00:02:00,360
day at three kilometres.

68
00:02:00,360 --> 00:02:01,200
Nia sits back down.

69
00:02:01,800 --> 00:02:03,020
And you remember something you read.

70
00:02:03,640 --> 00:02:04,280
In 2022,

71
00:02:04,900 --> 00:02:07,300
NASA crashed a spacecraft into an asteroid on

72
00:02:07,300 --> 00:02:07,680
purpose.

73
00:02:08,040 --> 00:02:08,800
It was called DART.

74
00:02:09,260 --> 00:02:10,740
It hit a rock about the same size

75
00:02:10,740 --> 00:02:13,540
as ours, at twenty-two thousand kilometres an hour.

76
00:02:13,540 --> 00:02:15,960
And it changed the rock's orbit by thirty-two

77
00:02:15,960 --> 00:02:16,440
minutes.

78
00:02:17,180 --> 00:02:19,040
Humans have already moved an asteroid.

79
00:02:19,820 --> 00:02:21,460
So we can too, you say.

80
00:02:22,220 --> 00:02:22,860
Kai looks at you.

81
00:02:23,520 --> 00:02:24,500
Then we don't stop it.

82
00:02:25,020 --> 00:02:26,040
We nudge it.

83
00:02:27,060 --> 00:02:29,000
So what would you do if you saw

84
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:29,340
it coming?

85
00:02:29,860 --> 00:02:30,480
Tell us in the comments.

86
00:02:30,480 --> 00:02:33,020
And subscribe, because next time, the five of

87
00:02:33,020 --> 00:02:34,920
you try to move a rock from space.

88
00:02:35,460 --> 00:02:36,960
And you find out what that would really

89
00:02:36,960 --> 00:02:37,320
take.
